<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al-Kitāb al-mukhtaṣar fī ḥisāb al-jabr wal-muqābala</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">820 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as al-Kitāb al-mukhtaṣar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Kitab al-mukhtasar fi hisab al-jabr wal-muqabala, the Compendious Book on Calculation by Completion and Balancing, is a mathematical treatise by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-khwarizmi">al-Khwarizmi</a> written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> around 820 CE. It set out systematic methods for solving linear and quadratic equations, and the word <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algebra">algebra</a> derives from al-jabr in its title. It is one of the best-known scientific achievements of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-kitab-al-mukhtasar-fi-hisab-al-jabr-wal-muqabala.jpg" alt="Al-Kitāb al-mukhtaṣar fī ḥisāb al-jabr wal-muqābala" width="240" height="380" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Muḥammad ibn Musa al-Khwarizmi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Image-Al-Kit%C4%81b_al-mu%E1%B8%ABta%E1%B9%A3ar_f%C4%AB_%E1%B8%A5is%C4%81b_al-%C4%9Fabr_wa-l-muq%C4%81bala.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Al-Jabr, also called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algebra">Algebra</a>, is an Arabic-language treatise on algebra written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> around 820 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-khwarizmi">al-Khwarizmi</a>; its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> title is Liber Algebræ et Almucabola. It gave an exhaustive account of solving for the positive roots of polynomial equations up to the second degree, and it was the first text to teach elementary algebra and the first to teach algebra for its own sake. The historian Victor J. Katz regards it as the first true algebra text that survives. The word algebra comes from the title's al-jabr, borrowed into Medieval Latin as algebrāica. Several later authors also published books under this title, among them Abu Kamil and Šarafaddīn al-Ṭūsī.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The book compiled and extended known rules for solving quadratic equations and some other problems. It introduced two operations. Al-jabr, meaning restoring or forcing, moves a deficient quantity to the other side of an equation, so that, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-khwarizmi">al-Khwarizmi</a>'s example, x² = 40x − 4x² becomes 5x² = 40x. Al-muqābala, balancing, subtracts the same positive quantity from both sides.</p><p>Together they eliminate negative quantities and leave squares, roots and numbers appearing at most once, which shows that there are only six basic solvable types when all coefficients and solutions are positive. Islamic mathematicians, unlike Indian ones, did not deal with negative numbers. As Carl Boyer notes, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algebra">algebra</a> is thoroughly rhetorical: equations are described in words, and even numbers are written out in words rather than symbols.</p><p>A second chapter catalogues methods of finding area and volume, including three approximations of pi, one of them 3.1416, which had appeared earlier in the Indian Āryabhaṭīya of 499. The book also explains the Jewish calendar and its 19-year cycle, and about half of it deals with the complex Islamic rules of inheritance, which require skill in first-order equations.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Robert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chester">Chester</a> translated the work into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> in 1145, and until the sixteenth century it was the principal mathematical textbook of European universities. Rashed and Angela Armstrong write that it differs from Babylonian tablets and from Diophantus' Arithmetica in treating the equation itself as the object of study, defining an infinite class of problems. O'Connor and Robertson call it a revolutionary move away from the Greek view of mathematics as essentially geometry. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algebra">Algebra</a> is now taught worldwide as part of school curricula.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Al-Jabr” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Al-Khwarizmi's treatise on calculation by completion and balancing (<b>al-Kitāb al-mukhtaṣar</b> fī ḥisāb al-jabr wal-muqābala, c. 820), the work that gave algebra its name, was written in this environment and permanently changed mathematics.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
